### What this guide helps you do

Use this guide to request access, sign in and start using GitHub Copilot in your browser or development
environment. Complete the preparation below, then follow the section for VS Code or Visual Studio. You only
need the tools approved for your role.

This guide covers GitHub Copilot for software development. Access to Microsoft 365 Copilot in Teams, Outlook
or Word does not by itself give you GitHub Copilot access.

### Before you start

- Use the GitHub account approved for your DfE work. Know its username and add and verify your own
  education.gov.uk email address in GitHub Settings > Emails, subject to approved DfE account policy.

- Ensure your DfE Microsoft Entra sign in and required MFA work. Complete organisation SSO where required,
  using the official organisation link supplied by DfE.

- Complete the required GitHub, security and Copilot training and read the GitHub user and single sign on
  guidance. Confirm that you understand the data handling and acceptable use requirements.

- Ask the approved access team to assign a DfE funded Copilot licence to the correct GitHub username.
  Organisation membership and successful SSO do not by themselves assign a Copilot licence.

- Use an approved device and supported, DfE approved software version. If installation is restricted, request
  software through the approved IT route.

### Request access if you do not have it

Use the published DfE access request route. Include your name, DfE email, exact GitHub username, team or
organisation, business reason and training status. The local request form and approval route must be confirmed
before publication.

You can check https://github.com/settings/copilot for entitlement or a request access option, where available.
Follow DfE approval requirements even if GitHub offers a self service request. Do not buy a personal plan or
start a free trial as a workaround.

### How to recognise success

You are signed into the approved GitHub account, the DfE funded entitlement is confirmed, and Copilot can
answer a safe test question in an authorised client. This does not confirm that every model, agent or
connected tool is approved.

## Sign in and open Copilot in your browser

### Step one Check your GitHub account

1. Open https://github.com and sign in to the GitHub account approved for DfE work.

2. Select your profile picture and check the username. Your GitHub username may differ from your DfE email
  address.

3. If you are signed into an account other than the one approved for your DfE work, switch to the approved
  account. If you do not know which account to use, contact the access team before linking identities.

### Step two Complete organisation SSO where required

1. Open the official SSO link for the organisation you need, or follow the SSO prompt shown by GitHub.

2. When redirected to Microsoft Entra ID, select your DfE work identity and complete the required MFA checks.

3. Check that the correct GitHub account is being linked. If GitHub says the identity is linked to another
  account, stop and contact support. Do not unlink it or create a replacement account yourself.

4. Return to GitHub and check access to a repository you are authorised to use. SSO and Copilot licensing are
  separate checks.

### Step three Open Copilot Chat

1. Go to https://github.com/copilot while signed into the approved account.

2. If prompted to subscribe, pay or use a free plan, check your username and ask the access team to confirm
  your DfE entitlement.

3. If a model selector is available, select an option on the current DfE approved model list. Use Auto only
  where approved. A model appearing in the interface does not confirm DfE approval.

4. Enter a safe test question, such as: Explain the difference between a C# class and an interface. Do not
  include personal data, secrets or sensitive project details.

Expected result: Copilot returns an answer. Feature availability depends on your entitlement and applicable
policy. If browser chat is disabled, use an approved IDE route or ask support; do not bypass the restriction.

### Before opening work content

Read the current GitHub guidance and check that your proposed data and use case are permitted. Copilot may use
repository, file or conversation context. Only open or attach information that is approved for this service.

## Set up Copilot in Visual Studio Code

Use this section for VS Code. Visual Studio is a different application and has separate instructions in the
next section. Button labels can vary with the installed version.

### Step one Start setup

1. Open the DfE approved version of Visual Studio Code.

2. Hover over the Copilot icon in the Status Bar and select Use AI Features. Follow the GitHub sign in
  prompts. Current VS Code setup installs the required extensions automatically. If the control is missing or
  installation is blocked, contact IT.

3. For DfE organisations hosted on github.com, use the GitHub.com sign in option. Do not choose a GHE.com
  instance unless your administrator has explicitly provided one.

### Step two Authorise the correct account

1. In the browser window opened by VS Code, check that the GitHub username matches the one with your DfE
  Copilot entitlement.

2. Review the authorisation prompt. Continue only if you initiated the sign in and it is the expected VS Code
  GitHub authentication flow.

3. Complete any required organisation SSO prompts, then allow the browser to return to Visual Studio Code when
  asked.

4. Check the Accounts menu in VS Code to confirm the GitHub account in use. If several accounts are present,
  confirm Copilot uses the licensed DfE approved one.

### Step three Open chat and test it

1. Open Copilot Chat using the Copilot or Chat button. On Windows or Linux, Ctrl+Alt+I opens chat in the
  standard keymap; use the menu if your shortcuts differ.

2. For your first test, choose Ask or an equivalent question only mode if offered. Avoid granting tools or
  running an agent for the initial check.

3. Select a DfE approved model if the selector is available, or an approved Auto option.

4. Ask: Explain what a unit test is in C# using a short example. Review the answer.

Expected result: chat responds without requesting a personal subscription. Check the Copilot status menu for
sign in, availability and usage information.

### Optional Check inline suggestions

In an approved practice project, open a C# file and write a simple comment describing a method. If an inline
suggestion appears, read it first. Tab commonly accepts the suggestion and Esc dismisses it; your keymap may
differ. If no suggestion appears, check the Copilot status and whether inline suggestions are enabled. Do not
change centrally managed policy.

## Set up Copilot in Visual Studio

Use this section for the full Visual Studio IDE. These steps follow Microsoft guidance for supported Visual
Studio versions; labels can differ between versions.

### Step one Check installation

1. Open your DfE approved Visual Studio installation and open a solution, project or the IDE without code.

2. Look for the GitHub Copilot badge in the upper right of the IDE. If Copilot is missing, use the approved
  software installation route. Microsoft provides an Install Copilot option and installer component, but local
  installation rights may be restricted.

3. Complete any required approved installation or update and restart Visual Studio when prompted.

### Step two Add your licensed GitHub account

1. Use the Visual Studio account menu to add or sign into a GitHub account. Your Microsoft or Visual Studio
  subscription sign in alone is not the GitHub Copilot sign in.

2. When the browser opens, check the exact GitHub username. Use the account assigned the DfE funded Copilot
  licence.

3. Complete the expected GitHub authorisation flow and any required organisation SSO, then return to Visual
  Studio.

4. Check the Copilot badge for your sign in or access status. If it offers a personal purchase or free plan,
  ask support to check the entitlement rather than subscribing.

### Step three Open chat

1. Select the GitHub Copilot badge, then Open Chat Window. Alternatively, use View > GitHub Copilot Chat where
  available.

2. Use a question only mode for the first test if offered. Choose a DfE approved model or approved Auto option
  where available.

3. Ask: Explain the difference between a C# class and an interface. Check that an answer appears.

Expected result: you can open chat and receive an answer using the approved account. If it fails, check the
GitHub account in Visual Studio and refresh its sign in before changing any configuration.

### Use it with your solution

Only open a repository or solution that is approved for use with Copilot. For the Developer Platform
repository, open the solution file in Visual Studio, then select a non sensitive code file and ask for an
explanation. Do not accept generated edits until you have reviewed the proposed changes and can test them.

## Use Copilot safely and get help

### A safe first task

1. Open a non sensitive practice file or an approved work file. Check the context or attachments included in
  chat.

2. Ask a focused question, such as: Explain this method and suggest test cases. Do not change any files yet.

3. Check the explanation against the code. Ask for clarification if it is wrong or incomplete.

4. If you request an edit, review the diff, build and run relevant tests. Follow the repository PR and human
  review process.

### Before approving actions

- Complete the required training and use only approved features and connections. Copilot access does not
  itself authorise access to another service.

- Review requested commands and tool actions. Do not approve unexpected access, external data transfers,
  deployment or security changes.

- Keep credentials, recovery codes and private keys out of prompts and screenshots. Follow the published usage
  allowance and request approval for exceptions.

### If something does not work

**Copilot asks for payment.** Check your GitHub username and ask the access team to verify that the DfE
entitlement is assigned to that exact account.

**SSO or MFA fails.** Use the official organisation SSO link and confirm your DfE sign in works. Send the
error and correlation ID to support without including credentials.

**Works in browser but not IDE.** Check the IDE GitHub account and software version. Save your work before
signing out and back into GitHub or restarting the IDE. This may also affect Git access.

**A model or feature is missing.** Check the published approved list and ask support about policy. Do not
switch accounts, add a personal API key or disable controls.

**Network or installation error.** Ask IT to check approved software, proxy and network configuration. Do not
disable certificate validation, firewall or device protections.

### What to include in a support request

Provide your GitHub username, DfE email, organisation, IDE and version, time of failure, exact sanitised error
and whether browser access works. Never send passwords, tokens, private keys or recovery codes. Use the
published DfE support route; confirm the correct service desk or mailbox for Copilot requests with your access team.